  "text": "The silence in the observation deck was not empty, but heavy, a physical weight that pressed against Elias’s temples. He sat in the center of the circular room, his hands resting on his knees, fingers twitching with a residual tremor that the dampening fields had not yet fully suppressed. Around him, the other candidates sat in similar postures of suspended animation, their eyes closed, their breathing synchronized to a slow, rhythmic pulse that emanated from the floor. It was a hypnotic beat, a lullaby for the dying self. Elias tried to recall the face of his daughter, the last person he had spoken to before the summons, but the image was already blurring, the edges softening like watercolor in rain. The system did not want memories; it wanted data. It wanted the raw, unfiltered input of a human mind, stripped of the narrative overlays that made life bearable. He remembered the argument with the technician, the way the man’s voice had been devoid of empathy, a flat drone that spoke of efficiency and compliance. \"Resistance is inefficient,\" the technician had said, his eyes scanning Elias’s biometrics with cold precision. \"Integration is the only logical path to stability. The chaos of individuality is a bug, not a feature. We are here to fix the code.\" Elias had wanted to scream, to throw his wristband into the sterile white void, to run back to the messy, painful, beautiful chaos of his old life. But his legs had not moved. His body had already begun to listen to the hum, to find comfort in the absence of choice. The door behind him hissed open, a soft exhalation of pressurized air. A figure in a white coat stepped forward, holding a tablet that glowed with a soft, blue light. \"Subject 742,\" the voice said, calm and detached. \"Your neural pathways are ready. The transition can begin. Do you accept the terms of your new existence?\" Elias looked at the figure, then at the others, who remained still as statues. He felt a strange, terrifying lightness in his chest, as if a heavy stone had been lifted. He nodded, a small, involuntary movement. The blue light on the tablet flared brighter, and the hum in the room grew louder, rising to a crescendo that drowned out his own heartbeat."
