  "text": "The sterile white walls of the intake facility seemed to expand and contract with every breath Elias took, a subtle optical illusion designed to induce a state of passive compliance. He stood in the queue, a line of identical grey jumpsuits stretching back into the dimly lit corridor, each person a hollow vessel waiting to be filled with purpose. The air smelled of ozone and antiseptic, a scent that had long since replaced the earthy, chaotic aroma of the city he had left behind. He remembered the rain that morning, the way it had slicked the pavement, reflecting the neon signs of a world that still believed in individual choice. That world felt like a dream now, distant and fragile. The technician at the front of the line had not looked up when Elias approached, his fingers dancing across a holographic interface with mechanical precision. \"Name?\" the voice asked, devoid of inflection. \"Elias Thorne,\" he replied, his voice sounding foreign to his own ears, thin and reedy. \"ID number?\" \"742-Alpha-9." The technician paused, a micro-second delay that felt like an eternity. \"Biometric scan required. Place your wrist on the pad.\" Elias complied, the cold surface of the scanner biting into his skin. A red laser swept across his palm, mapping the intricate web of veins and fingerprints. He watched the data stream across the screen, numbers and codes cascading in a waterfall of information. He tried to think of his daughter, Maya, her laughter echoing in his mind, but the sound was already fading, replaced by the low, thrumming hum of the facility’s power core. The technician finally looked up, his eyes flat and unseeing. \"Integration sequence initiated. Please proceed to the observation deck. Do not resist the transition. Resistance is inefficient. Integration is the only logical path to stability. The chaos of individuality is a bug, not a feature. We are here to fix the code.\" Elias felt a cold dread settle in his stomach, but his feet moved forward