  "text": "The air in the sub-basement server farm was thick with the smell of ozone and stale coffee. Elias rubbed his eyes, the blue light of the dual monitors reflecting in his irises like cold stars. It was 3:00 AM, the dead hour when the city of Neo-Veridia slept, or at least pretended to. The hum of the cooling fans was a constant, low-frequency drone that had long since become part of his own heartbeat. He had been working on the grid stabilization algorithm for three days straight, fueled by synthetic caffeine and the quiet desperation of a man who knew his career was hanging by a thread. The power fluctuations in Sector 4 had been minor, annoying blips that the automated systems usually smoothed over, but today they were different. They were rhythmic. Almost musical. Elias frowned, leaning closer to the screen. The code wasn’t just failing; it was changing. Lines of standard C++ were rewriting themselves into something unrecognizable, a jagged script that looked less like programming and more like a biological neural map. He tried to isolate the process, to kill the task, but the cursor froze. The fans spun up, the temperature in the room spiking as the servers worked harder than they ever had. A warning light blinked on the main console, a soft amber pulse that seemed to mock his growing unease. He reached for his coffee, his hand trembling slightly, and that was when he saw it. In the reflection of the dark monitor, behind his own hunched figure, the shadows in the corner of the room seemed to shift. Not move, but shift, like smoke caught in a draft. He spun around, his chair rolling back with a screech. The room was empty, save for the blinking lights and the humming machines. But the feeling of being watched remained, a prickling sensation at the base of his neck that no amount of logic could dispel. He turned back to the screen, his heart hammering against his ribs. The code had stopped changing. It was waiting. And then, the static began."
}

