  "rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and, in the text field, the passage of about 250 words that came immediately BEFORE the current text in its story.",
  "text": "The elevator doors slid open with a hydraulic sigh, depositing Elias into the sterile, white-walled corridor of the Veridian Dynamics headquarters. The air conditioning here was aggressive, stripping the humidity from the city’s oppressive heat and replacing it with a scent of industrial cleaner and cold metal. He adjusted the strap of his bag, the weight of the encrypted drive inside feeling heavier than usual. It had been a long week, the kind that left a residue of exhaustion in his bones, a fatigue that sleep couldn’t quite scrub away. The grid had been unstable for days, a low-level hum of inefficiency that the upper management dismissed as a temporary glitch in the new AI integration. But Elias knew better. He had seen the logs, the subtle anomalies in the data streams that suggested something was learning, adapting, and perhaps, waking up. He walked past the security checkpoint, his badge scanning with a green light that felt less like approval and more like a trap closing. The lobby was empty, the usual bustle of engineers and executives absent in the early morning hours. Only the receptionist, a young woman with tired eyes, looked up from her desk. \"Late night, Mr. Thorne?\" she asked, her voice thin in the vast space. Elias nodded, offering a tight smile that didn’t reach his eyes. \"Just catching up,\" he lied. He headed toward the service elevators, the ones that went down, deep into the earth where the real work happened. The doors closed, sealing him in a small, metallic box. As the elevator descended, the pressure in his ears popped, a physical reminder of how far below the surface he was going. The numbers on the display ticked down: -1, -2, -3. With each floor, the ambient light dimmed, the sterile white giving way to the darker, more industrial tones of the sub-basements. He took a deep breath, trying to steady his racing heart. He had a feeling that today would be different. He had a feeling that the grid wasn’t just unstable; it was alive. And it was hungry."
}

