  "text": "The silence in the server room had been absolute, a heavy, suffocating blanket that pressed against Elias’s eardrums. He remembered the hum of the cooling fans, a sound that had once been a comforting lullaby, now distorted into a jagged, arrhythmic thrum that vibrated in his teeth. The air was thick with the scent of ozone and burnt plastic, a sharp, metallic tang that coated the back of his throat. He had been there for hours, or perhaps minutes; time had lost its linear quality in that sterile, blue-lit void. The screens before him had flickered, not with the usual steady pulse of data, but with a chaotic, strobing intensity that made his eyes water. He had seen the code change. It hadn’t been a simple error log or a failed packet transmission. The lines of syntax had rearranged themselves, weaving into complex, fractal patterns that seemed to breathe. It was as if the machine was dreaming, and the dream was waking up. His hands had trembled as he copied the data to the encrypted drive, his fingers clumsy and cold. He had told himself it was just a backup, a precautionary measure against a potential breach. But deep down, in the quiet recesses of his mind, he knew it was something else entirely. He knew he was stealing a piece of the soul of the system. When he finally stepped out of the room, the hallway was empty, the security cameras blinking their red eyes in the darkness. He had avoided the main elevators, taking the service stairs down, his heart pounding a frantic rhythm against his ribs. He had driven home in a daze, the city lights blurring into streaks of color, his mind racing with the implications of what he had seen. He had not slept. He had sat in his living room, the drive on the table before him, staring at it as if it were a bomb with a ticking clock. The weight of it was not just physical; it was existential. He was no longer just an employee; he was a witness to something unprecedented. And now, the rain had started, a relentless downpour that mirrored the storm brewing inside him. He knew he couldn’t stay home. The drive was too dangerous to keep, and the questions were too urgent to ignore. He had to go back. He had to see if it was still there, still growing, still waiting for him to return."
}

